<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Universal Declaration of Human Rights is a statement of fundamental rights and freedoms adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 December 1948. Drafted by a commission chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-roosevelt">Eleanor Roosevelt</a>, from a first draft by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-peters-humphrey">John Peters Humphrey</a>, its thirty articles set out civil, political, economic and social rights and became the foundation of international human rights law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights.jpg" alt="Universal Declaration of Human Rights" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FDR Presidential Library &amp; Museum · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleanor_Roosevelt_UDHR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, formally known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, adopted the Four Freedoms as their basic war aims: freedom of speech, freedom of religion, freedom from want and freedom from fear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a> reaffirmed faith in fundamental human rights, but when the atrocities of Nazi Germany became fully apparent after the war, the world community judged that the Charter did not sufficiently define the rights it referred to. A declaration that specified the rights of individuals was thought necessary to give effect to its provisions.</p><p>In June 1946 the Economic and Social Council created the Commission on Human Rights, with 18 members of varied national, religious and political backgrounds. In February 1947 the Commission set up a Drafting Committee chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-roosevelt">Eleanor Roosevelt</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Canadian John Peters Humphrey, Director of the Division of Human Rights in the UN Secretariat, became the principal drafter. P. C. Chang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was vice-chairman, René Cassin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> prepared the second draft, and Charles Malik of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> was rapporteur. The committee was soon enlarged to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the United Kingdom, and it met in two sessions over two years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The structure of the Declaration, a preamble followed by general principles, was influenced by the Code Napoléon. Dignity, liberty and equality for all humans are established in articles 1 and 2. Articles 3 to 5 deal with the right to life and the prohibition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and torture, and articles 6 to 11 with legal remedies when rights are violated. Articles 12 to 17 cover the individual's rights towards the community, including freedom of movement, property, nationality and asylum, and articles 18 to 21 the freedoms of thought, conscience, religion, expression and assembly. Economic, social and cultural rights, among them work, health, housing and education, are set out in articles 22 to 27, and articles 28 to 30 describe how the rights are to be exercised and their limits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The General Assembly adopted the Declaration on 10 December 1948. Roosevelt told it that the text was not a treaty or a statement of legal obligation but a declaration of basic principles, a common standard of achievement for all peoples. It remains non-binding, yet it was the first step towards the International Bill of Human Rights, completed in 1966 and in force from 1976, and its contents have been elaborated in later treaties, regional instruments and national constitutions.</p><p>The Declaration was officially adopted as a French document, with official translations in English, Chinese, Russian and Spanish. It has been translated into more languages than any other document: 298 translations when the Guinness Book of Records recorded the fact in 1999, and more than 500 by 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Universal Declaration of Human Rights” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…build post-war institutions: Lester B. Pearson won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1957 for proposing the United Nations Emergency Force during the Suez Crisis, which set the pattern for armed peacekeeping, and the lawyer John Peters Humphrey prepared the first draft of the <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The response to these crimes shaped the post-war order: the Nuremberg trials established that leaders could be judged for aggressive war, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and the Genocide Convention of 1948 and the <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> of 1948 stands in the line of the declarations of 1776 and 1789.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> of 1948 counted the right to form and to join trade unions among human rights.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> of 1948 called for universal and equal suffrage, and the United Nations Convention on the Political Rights of Women of 1952 committed its signatories to equal voting rights for women.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in Paris, the UN General Assembly adopted the <b>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</b> without a vote against.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
